That sounds like a worthwhile goal. The good news is that we already have some solid defenses in place — antivirus software runs regularly, endpoints run endpoint protection tools, and organizations use zero-trust approaches to limit lateral movement. The real challenge is staying ahead of the curve; malware evolves constantly, exploits become more sophisticated, and detection becomes harder. If you're looking to strengthen your own security posture, there are several concrete actions that will make a real difference:

- Keep software updated — Windows and macOS get patches regularly; older systems need updates;
- Enable multi-factor authentication everywhere possible;
- Use a reputable endpoint protection platform (EEPP) with real-time behavioral monitoring;
- Don't open suspicious links, download attachments, or click embedded URLs from unknown sources;
- Keep systems patched against known vulnerabilities;
- Back up data regularly and test restoration procedures;
- Consider working with a security consultant if you're managing a large network.

If you want to dig into specific topics (endpoint protection, zero-trust architecture, or how EDR works), I can expand on those.
